export const edition='morarg-argument-5.2';
export const thesis='Do we need objective moral facts to settle how we treat each other? This exhibit argues that we don’t. Facts about the world never tell us, on their own, what we should do. The reasons we can give the people affected, plus an honest look at what is influencing our judgment, can do the work we need morality to do. The exhibit does not try to prove that objective moral facts don’t exist. It argues that we never need to rely on them, and it says plainly where that claim is weakest.';
export const framing='Each room tests one job that objective moral facts are said to be needed for. You decide each time. At the end you will see your own ledger of jobs and your answers to the opening questions side by side.';
export const brief=[
 ['The question','When people disagree about how to treat each other, do we need objective moral facts (truths that hold whatever anyone thinks or would agree to) to settle it?'],
 ['The claim','No. Facts tell us how things are; what we should do also needs a value. Two things supply what we need: the reasons we can give the people a choice affects, reasons they could accept if well informed, free to object and treated as equals; and an honest look at the influences on judgment, separating those that carry information from those that only push.'],
 ['What it claims, exactly','That in explaining our moral judgments, justifying decisions to one another and protecting people, we never need to rely on objective moral facts. It does not claim to prove they don’t exist. A realist can accept the same reasons and safeguards and say they are correct because they track objective truths. The exhibit’s argument against that extra layer is that, if nothing in our practice depends on it, it does no work.'],
 ['How it is tested','Objectivists say their facts are needed for particular jobs: explaining our moral feelings, getting from facts to what we should do, making cruelty more than taste, settling disagreement, showing a whole society can be wrong, ruling out terrible agreements, binding people who don’t care, and making sense of our own reasoning. Each gets a room, plus three real cases.'],
 ['Where it is weakest','Ruling out terrible agreements, where the answer rests on a commitment (answer each person, don’t add up small gains against one person’s agony) that an objectivist says must be objectively true. And duties to people who cannot take part, such as future generations and animals.']
];
export const glossary=[
 ['Objective moral fact','A moral truth that would hold no matter what anyone thinks, feels or would agree to, even if everyone, reasoning carefully and fairly, concluded otherwise. Philosophers call belief in such facts moral realism; this exhibit calls its supporters objectivists.'],
 ['Reasons between people','The view that what we owe each other is settled by the reasons we can give the people our choices affect: reasons they could accept if they were well informed, free to object and treated as equals. It is not a vote, and not whatever people happen to agree to today. This exhibit adapts ideas from T. M. Scanlon and Jürgen Habermas; Scanlon himself is a realist about reasons, so the adaptation is the exhibit’s own.'],
 ['Influences','Anything that changes how someone judges. Some influences carry reasons: new information, someone’s testimony, a real change in what is possible or who is burdened. Others only push: fear stirred by a misleading story, obedience to authority, wanting to fit in, self-interest dressed up as principle. The same event can do both. The useful question is: did this change what is true or possible, or only how we feel about it?'],
 ['Pressures','Influences that only push: they change judgment without changing the reasons.'],
 ['Is and ought','A fact says how things are (“smoking shortens lives”). An “ought” says what should be done (“you should stop”). Getting from the first to the second always needs something more: a want, a value, a commitment.'],
 ['A job','Something objectivists say morality cannot do without objective facts. Jobs differ in kind: explaining why people judge as they do, justifying a decision, protecting people, or making sense of reasoning. For each job you say whether it needs objective facts.'],
 ['Your ledger','Your answers on each job, collected at the end. If any job truly needs objective facts, the exhibit’s claim fails there.'],
 ['Principle','A plain sentence you can choose to carry. Your opening answers pre-select some: those principles are your own opening answers, word for word or as their plain opposite. Kept principles appear beside later decisions they bear on.']
];
export const badges={Situation:'A concrete case to think with.',Definition:'An idea used throughout, explained in plain terms.',Evidence:'A documented fact or research finding, within its stated limits.','For objectivity':'The strongest case that this job needs objective moral facts.','Without objectivity':'The best answer that does the job without them.','Still open':'What neither side has settled.',Influences:'What may have shaped people’s judgment: some carry reasons, some only push.',Reasons:'What could, or could not, be justified to the people affected.',Model:'An illustrative model, with authored assumptions rather than measurements.'};
